<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bali Aga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bali Mula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> Aga, or Bali Mula, are Balinese communities that preserve customs older than the Hindu-Javanese court culture that spread on Bali after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> conquest of the 14th century. They live mainly in mountain villages such as Trunyan on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-batur">Lake Batur</a> and Tenganan in eastern Bali, with distinctive village councils, burial practices and rituals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bali-aga.jpg" alt="Bali Aga" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Hazzard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geringsingi_texttile_in_use.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
